# Repeat Until Condition Fails

A **while loop** keeps executing its body as long as a condition is true.

```csharp
while (condition)
{
    // code to repeat
}
```

Example: countdown from 5.
```csharp
int i = 5;
while (i > 0)
{
    Debug.Log(i);
    i--; // decrease i each time
}
```

**Important**: If the condition never becomes false, you get an **infinite loop** and Unity will freeze.

# Escaping a Loop

An infinite loop happens when the condition never becomes false:
```csharp
while (true) { } // freezes Unity!
```

To stop a loop early, use the **break** statement:
```csharp
while (true)
{
    if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Escape))
        break; // exit the loop
}
```

**Continue** skips the rest of the current iteration and goes to the next check:
```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
{
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue; // skip evens
    Debug.Log(i); // prints odds
}
```

# Execute at Least Once

A **do-while** loop is like while, but it checks the condition **after** the body runs.

```csharp
do
{
    // code
} while (condition);
```

Use it when you need the code to execute at least once, regardless of the condition. Example:
```csharp
int number;
do
{
    number = Random.Range(1, 10);
} while (number != 7); // keep trying until 7 is rolled
```

